{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>And he took from them Shimon.</b> He detained Shimon more than all the rest, in order to separate him from Levi, so that the two of them would not take counsel together against him — in the manner they did against Shekhem — for the two of them were always found together, as it is written (Genesis 49:5): ״שמעון ולוי אחים כלי חמס״, etc. And what is written (v. 27), ״ויפתח האחד את שקו״ — this is Levi; and because Shimon had been separated from him, Scripture called him 'one.' And so Yosef needed to take him from them, but they did not take him. From here our Rabbis of blessed memory learned concerning gentiles who say to Israel: 'Give us one of you and we will kill him, and if not we will kill all of you' — they must fight and all be killed rather than surrender even one of Israel to execution. But if they singled out one of them on their own and said, 'Give us so-and-so,' then they do give him to them, and they should not all be killed. For so we find with Sheva ben Bikhri, who rose against David, as it is written (II Samuel 20:1): ושם נקרא איש בליעל ושמו שבע בן בכרי איש ימיני ויתקע בשופר ויאמר אין לנו חלק בדוד ולא נחלה לנו בבן ישי איש לאהליו ישראל. And when he fled and entered Abel of Beth-Maakhah, Yoav pursued him, as it is written: ויבאו ויצורו עליו באבלה בית המעכה וישפכו סללה אל העיר ותעמוד בחל וכל העם אשר את יואב משחיתים להפיל החומה, and Yoav said to them — a man of Mount Ephraim, Sheva ben Bikhri is his name, who lifted his hand against the king, against David — 'Give him up alone and I will withdraw from the city,' and the woman said to Yoav: 'Behold, his head is thrown to you over the wall.'"], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 42, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:19:59.949174217Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}